      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "AlCoFeNi is predominantly ferromagnetic because of its Co content.",
      "field": "material_initial_state",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6197204::chunk::000::abstract::a6951d7e3c"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Partially replacing Co with Cr in AlCoFeNi increases coercive field.",
      "field": "hypothesis",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6197204::chunk::000::abstract::a6951d7e3c"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Al(Co0.5Cr0.5)FeNi is an example of partial Co-to-Cr substitution in AlCoFeNi.",
      "field": "intervention",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6197204::chunk::000::abstract::a6951d7e3c"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Cr has antiferromagnetic tendencies whereas Co is ferromagnetic.",
      "field": "intervention",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6197204::chunk::000::abstract::a6951d7e3c"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "The coercivity increase should occur at room temperature, and potentially at cryogenic temperatures.",
      "field": "conditions",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The coercivity increase should hold while the Cr-substituted alloy remains a single solid solution without secondary phases.",
      "field": "conditions",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Co-to-Cr substitution increases magnetic inhomogeneity by creating local antiferromagnetic Cr regions in a ferromagnetic matrix.",
      "field": "structural_mediator",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The local magnetic inhomogeneity enhances domain-wall pinning.",
      "field": "structural_mediator",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Cr substitution creates competing ferromagnetic and antiferromagnetic exchange interactions.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6197204::chunk::000::abstract::a6951d7e3c"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Competing magnetic interactions act as domain-wall pinning centers.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Greater domain-wall pinning requires a higher applied field to reorient magnetization and therefore raises coercivity.",
      "field": "mechanism",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Cr-substituted alloy will have higher coercivity than unsubstituted AlCoFeNi.",
      "field": "predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6197204::chunk::000::abstract::a6951d7e3c"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Cr-substituted alloy will show only a modest reduction in saturation magnetization.",
      "field": "predicted_outcome",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6197204::chunk::000::abstract::a6951d7e3c"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Unsubstituted AlCoFeNi has lower coercivity because its ferromagnetic exchange is more uniform.",
      "field": "baseline",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "partial",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6197204::chunk::000::abstract::a6951d7e3c"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
    },
    {
      "text": "The effect requires a Cr level low enough to avoid secondary-phase formation or excessive magnetic dilution.",
      "field": "boundary",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "unsupported",
      "supporting_ids": [],
      "same_system": "na"
    },
    {
      "text": "Cr substitution and lower annealing temperatures tend to decompose this alloy system into ordered B2 and disordered BCC phases.",
      "field": "supporting_evidence_ids",
      "needs_support": true,
      "support": "entailed",
      "supporting_ids": [
        "PMC6197204::chunk::000::abstract::a6951d7e3c"
      ],
      "same_system": "yes"
